From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: RANT: Big Science, NASA, and Killing Your Children

My collegue and friend Amara Graps has apparently taken offense at my
hyperbole in suggesting she find an "honest" job:

> >(Sorry, Robin and Amara, but it's time for you to find honest work.
> >Robin is going off to Caltech, so we're almost there. As for Dani, Nick, and
> 
> Rather arrogant of you to choose what career path is best for me, don'tcha
> think? I think that I make fine contributions to science where I'm at. I'm 
....

Rather arrogant of Amara to think I was being arrogant...whoops!

Gee, I had hoped my comments spoke for themselves and didn't need a "smiley."

Plenty of fine people at NASA, and at other pork troughs as well. And
plenty of turkeys, to mix barnyard metaphors, in private industry. The
average quality of NASA scientists is probably higher than at, say,
Intel. (This is to show my views are not coming from chauvinism.)

(Studies of this have shown that some people gravitate to the national
labs--Los Alamos, Livermore, Ames, etc.--because of a willingness to
trade off earnings growth for security, less pressure to publish (as
compared to university professors), etc. And I'm sure others basically
like the work and see no private industry alternatives.)

The problem is of course that if a private company has "bad memes" (a
bad plan, a bad way of doing business, or just bad luck), it dies out.
Competition 101, and all that. Government projects and agencies rarely
face this Darwinian discipline...witness the Big Science boondoggles
so many of us are decrying. Government programs that screw up big time
often get more funds to hide the screwup (as when the Challenger
Commission was attempting to obfuscate the causes--cold weather--until
Feynman deliberately created an unavoidable publicity stunt with his
O-ring in icewater).

I'll make a deal with Robin, Amara, and other NASA supporters: do to
NASA what was done to AT&T--split NASA into at least 4 competing
groups, with separate budgets, and kill off one of them every 5 years.
(A poor simulacrum of real business practice, but it's a start.)

Oh, and let private companies bid to take over the assets of these
sub-NASAs. Privatization of NASA, a la Thatcher and Mitterand.

Robin asked in another posting if I would sell Intel chips to NASA.
Sure! But I'd also vote to cut NASA funding in a New York minute
(whatever that is). I use the U.S. Postal Service for most mail, and I
would sell them chip, too, but I would choose to see all mail
privatized without any hesitation. That there are "good people" there
is irrelevant, just as it is at NASA.

Seriously, I don't fault Robin or Amara for working at or for NASA,
so long as it doesn't warp their judgment about the benefits of
Space Stations, monopolies on launches, and other such travesties.
 

> >The truly exciting science I've seen recently is being done on the
> >Keck Telescope, built largely with private funds and producing better
> 
> Are you so sure about the source of the funds? (By the way, the second
> Keck is being built, so there will soon be two of them). I know that the
> Keck Foundation supplied some large fraction of the costs, but I am
> also fairly sure that Univ of CA provided much additional funding.

The $94 million cost was split between the Keck Foundation, Caltech,
and the University of California, mainly. I don't know the exact
split, but the initial grant of $10 million came from the Keck
Foundation and allowed the builders a mostly free-from-pork-politics
hand at designing the scope. And it came in on time, under budget, and
so nearly perfect that the Keck Foundation kicked in (kecked in?) more
money to immediately begin construction of a twin, to be completed in
several years, as Amara noted above.

The corrective lenses alone for the Hobble could have built 2.5 more
Kecks! The storage costs for the mirror, for the half-decade it sat in
its defective glory awaiting launch, could have built several more.
The Hobble itself, at several billion, could have funded 40 such Keck
telescopes. And so on.

But the Hobble had many aerospace contractors, many Congressional
patrons, and was "Big Science." Hence the main support.

(To be fair to the Hubble, the lower sky brightness will allow fainter
stars and galaxies to be imaged in the CCDs. I'm hopeful other amazing
discoveries will be made by it. But the Hubble stand a very good
chance of being disabled by any one of several things: cosmic debris
strikes (including manmade junk), bumping of the mirror and supports
by astronauts, loss when/if it is ever returned to Earth for
servicing, deterioration of the electronics and computers, etc. Some
analysts give it 15 years, tops, and perhaps only several years. The
Keck scopes, on the other hand, should be in operation for many
decades, unless the volcano blows, and the components can be
continuously repaired and upgraded.)

The issue is not so much Keck vs. Hubble, as both systems have their
advantages and disadvantages, but "Medium Science" vs. "Big Science."
Private, or tight-loop, financing often produces smaller but more
elegant projects, while government, or open-loop, financing often
produces techno-pork and white elephants. Often so late in coming that
the needs have changed and the technology has moved on...look at the
electronics in the Shuttle for a frightening glimpse of this approach
at work.

-Tim May

