Subject: Sex Differences

Related to this, why aren't there more women on this list?
Usenet in general?  How many women were in the old Homebrew
Computer Club?  How many attend Cypherpunks meeting?  Cryonics?
(A few, but not nearly the proportion in the health field in general).  
It seems (I have seen no studies on this) that those social situations 
where people informally get together to share their interest in 
technology, as opposed to the officially sanctioned routines of
school, job, etc. are where women are the most underepresented.
Here's an overstereotyped version of that observation: Society of
Women Engineers get together to discuss Careers in Engineering,
while men informally get together to discuss Cool Tech and Neat Hacks.

This may reflect one or more of the following:  (1) some widespread 
subconcious (or at least self-deceptive) discrimination against
women in these kind of informal, emergent social groups 
by men. I've heard women complain about, eg being ignored during
discussions, but on the other hand in my experience techno-
sophisticated men tend to be more tolerant than the average male,
and quite often voice concern about the lack of participation
by women.  (2) Most women just are not interested in technology unless 
it's highly practical, personal, or relevent to their immediate situation, 
or it's deemed socially important (eg by official school classes, 
high-paying jobs, etc.).  (3) women just can't stand to be around, or 
tend to ignore, techno-geek boys (kind of a a reverse-sexist version of 
explanation (1)).  (4) women can't stand getting hit on so often.
(5) when a woman walks in the room, Cool Tech and Neat Hacks vanish
from the minds of most of the men, ending the hopes of
said woman to participate in that kind of discussion.

Now that I've offended all concerned, I'll humbly end this
round of speculation. :-)


