Subject: TECH: Zero Point Energy

"The Butterfly" writes:

>Well, a funny thing happened today at the ExI luncheon...
>        I asked if anyone could give me more info on Zero Point Energy, and
>got a bunch of blank stares and "What's that?" kinda looks, and it turned out
>that I was the only one who'd ever heard of it! :)

Well, I guess it's a good thing I didn't make the 50 mile drive over for
lunch, as I probably would've sounded negative and critical...in public, no
less. (Hal Finney and I teamed up earlier this year to convince Max More
not to commission a major article for "Extropy" on the coming Golden Age of
Zero Point Energy. Actually, a good article covering all aspects of this
case might be pretty interesting, and Max may still want to have this. But
the free energy hype is clearly quackery. Obviously, I could be wrong on
this, as with anything else, but this seems unlikely.)

The Casimir Effect cannot produce sustained energy release any more than
dropping a weight can produce sustained energy. Sure, two parallel plates
with vacuum between them want to come together, due to the Casimir effect,
and energy is released....once!  The kicker is you've got to pull them
apart again!

Hal Puthoff, previously well-known for his "remote-viewing" work on ESP,
with Targ and Uri Geller, only has speculations about how "wormholes"
coupling into the cosmic reservoir of free energy, can somehow "recycle"
the energy. Has anyone seen anything remotely like this? No. Where are the
measurements? They don't exist. How is this theory testable? Blank looks.

(And I mean "testable" with existing equipment or with foreseeable
refinements of existing equipment, as with the Bevatron example or the
eclipse example I cited in my posting about Robin's comments.)

I would take comments made by Puthoff in the same vein as the physics of
Jack Sarfatti. Set your bogosity detector on "sensitive" when these names
appear.

-Tim May


