Date: Thu, 10 Jun 93 18:38:32 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: QM: Theories vs. Interpretations

Robin Hanson writes:

>Hmm.. seems Tim May and I mean different things by "testable".  I mean
>"can be tested somehow someday", and Tim seems to mean "can be tested
>soon/now".  Folks are free to mean what they want by words, but most
>of the Popperian dictims regarding "testable" use my sense of the
>word, I think.

I don't buy that I'm misusing the word "testable." When Einstein predicted
that star fields would be affected differently by the sun than the
Newtonian theory predicted (by about a factor of 2), and that an eclipse
might be a good way to test this, he was stating how his theory could be
tested. The actual test came several years later, when Eddington and his
friends travelled to some locale to photograph such a total eclipse. The
shift predicted by Einstein was seen, and thus General Relativity was not
falsified (some would say it was confirmed).

What I wrote in my last post on this subject was:

"This is what I said. These different interpretations make no
different predictions, nothing that is falsifiable in a Popperian
sense. The advocates of the Foo Interpretation have not said "Our
interpretation predicts that Experiment X will produce Result Y. If it
does not, we stand ready to abandon our interpretation."

"No one has devised any test of the major interpretations. Could they
in the future? Sure. But they haven't yet."

I stand by this. At this time, by all that we know, the major
interpretations of QM are not distinguishable by today's tests or by
reasonable extensions of today's tests.

This is not playing with semantics--a would-be experimentalist in this area
would say: "Tell me what to measure. Tell me how Foo Interpretation will
yield different results than Bar Interpretation."

This "testability" can certainly involve equipment not yet in existence,
with some caution. When the Berkeley "Bevatron" was built in the mid-50s,
it had a specific goal of reaching the 6-7 GeV (formerly BeV) needed to
make antiprotons, according to the untested theory. The theorists said the
Bevatron would be a test of their theory, and the finding of the antiproton
in 1956 "confirmed" (or did not falsify, in Popperian terms) the theory.

Saying that someday, somehow, someone will devise a test is certainly
possible, but this does not a falsifiable theory make. If, for example, I
assert that ghostly spirits control the motions of subatomic particles, and
that I believe that someday a "ghost detector" will allow us to measure
whether ghosts are present or not, this surely would not make my theory a
"testable" one!

-Tim May

