Date: Fri, 11 Jun 93 02:23:27 -0400
From: hhuang@Athena.MIT.EDU
Subject: DIET: lactobacillus acidophilus, other gut ``good'' bacteria

I have been taking an acidophilus capsule every day in the morning,
and I believe has done me mild benefits.  My digestion is smoother; I
get less gas, for instance.  There are other similar benefits, the
grosser details of which I shall neglect to give.

Lactobacillus (acidophilus is not the only kind) is recommended in
some quarters to help prevent bacterial infections and fungal
diseases, particularly candidiasis, which occurs when the fungus
Candida proliferates out of control in the intestines, preventing
nutrient absorption, among other nasty things.  A common cause of
candidiasis is oral antibiotics, as antibiotics kill the good bacteria
in the body which help keep fungal diseases and other bad bacteria
from multiplying out of control.  Women in particular are at risk, as
vaginas are good breeding areas for fungal proliferation, yeast
infections being a better known example.  Another example I have
noticed is that almost, if not all, posters to sci.med who discuss
fungal growths under nails, have female names.  Finally, when I was
taking oral tetracycline for my acne for prolonged periods of time, I
developed athlete's foot, which is a fungal disease.

So for all these reasons, I have been taking acidophilus, having now
been convinced that having a weakened flora of good intestinal
bacteria is something that could be, and should be, easily remedied.
Furthermore, I am quitting dairy products for a while, so
supplementation becomes my sole source of bacterial culture.

Now it is said that the bacteria in our intestines can help or harm us
in our absorption.  Bad bacteria, such as having too much coliform
bacteria for example, will hamper digestion and absorption of
nutrients in the intestines, particularly the absorption of various
minerals, which are often difficult enough to absorb as it is.  My
question is,

Do different types of intestinal bacteria make a difference as to how
we absorb nutrients from our food?  There are many strains of
bacteria.  Is there an optimal strain, or group of strains, that one
ought to have?  In fact, it is recommended that one not take two
differing strains, as they might compete against one another, and thus
reduce the effectiveness if only one were to be taken.  And health
food stores also sell cultures with up to five (perhaps even more
exist) differing strains of bacteria.  So is there a ``right'' answer?

Also, one last bit of interesting material.  When a baby is born, it
has no bacteria in its gut.  Now you would think a newborn baby would
need a lot of nutritional iron to start making some blood.  Then why
does human breast milk contain almost zero amounts of iron?  You would
certainly think the newborn baby needs the iron for good blood.  The
answer is apparently because bacteria in culture grow rapidly when
there is iron present.  So in the first few days of the newborn, the
only kind of bacteria generally grows in the baby gut without iron is,
you guessed it, milk bacteria, or lactobacillus.  All other bacteria
can result in deadly infections and kill the newborn.  But without
iron, only the ``good'' lactobacillus can grow.  With the
lactobacillus in place, the baby now has a lot more protection from
the host of other deadly bacteria that exist on earth.

The moral of this is that one should be always be knowledgeable when
taking supplements.  I do not have references, but it has been
demonstrated that taking excessive iron supplementation increases
one's risk of bacterial infection.  So some people who pop lots of
iron pills every day, particularly women who have been frightened by
the iron deficiency and anemia scares, are apparently doing themselves
more harm than good.

While I am at it, I might as well say that I have read from
sci.med.nutrition that one of the million things that vitamin C does
is help in the process of converting bodily vitamin A (retinol) into
retinoic acid, which then augments the immune system in some of the
ways that vitamin C is purported to do.  The post said that people who
take too much C, without watching their intake of A, were doing
themselves more harm than good, as the C will drain the body's supply
of A, which is stored in the liver.  Most people have dangerously low
stores of vitamin A in their liver.  I have also read a referenced
sentence in _Mind Food and Smart Pills_ about how taking C seems to
avoid or prevent as many of the problems of vitamin A toxicity.  These
two sources would seem to match.  So perhaps this may explain why some
studies with megadoses of C may have results contrary to the expected
immune benefits.  A complete speculation on my part.

As I am not an expert on any of these matters, if any of the above is
wrong, please correct me quickly.

