From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: RANT: Big Science, NASA, and Killing Your Children

As sci.space readers well know, I'm in substantial agreement with Tim 
May on this one.  Tim, instead of preaching to the converted, how
about posting your rant in sci.space?  It's badly needed at this 
juncture, what with all the corrupt syncophants lobbying for the space 
station with taxpayer money.  Your comments about space-crystals and 
the quote from the Intel guy would be especially pertinent.  BTW, the 
latest big excuse for the space station is "life science", which as 
close as they can get to "astronauts on TV" without losing credibility 
even with the astronaut groupie crowd.

In my more cynical moments, I think the space station is a good
thing.  It's such a pathetic excuse for technology that
it serves as a shining example any time one wants to dump on government 
techno-pork.  The stuff I find dangerous is the stuff that actually
halfway makes sense, like the Data "Superhighway" and Sematech where
government comes in and plays havoc with emerging private markets.
Most dangerous of all is the stuff that gives government control
over information, like the "Superhighway" and key escrow wiretap
chips.  If the government wasted its money on Fred instead of
Clipper we'd all be much better off.

On nanotech I think many, to some extent even Drexler himself,
misunderstand its role.  It's not an emerging technology.  It's
not something for companies to go off and work in in today's
labs and make money.  It's a vision of the future.  Especially
with cryonics, it is becoming very important to know what kind 
of capabilities might be possible up to centuries in the 
future, even if in most respects it is almost totally unpredictable.

Nanotechnology, and more broadly theoretical applied science, fills
that role -- helps answer important questions like, "will we be able to 
revive suspendees?".  Theoretical applied science also helps answer
questions like the Fermi Paradox (what tech advanced civilizations might 
be capable of), what will life be like in 200 years (a world many of 
us expect to see), etc.  Such far-ranging thinking can also 
stimulate creativity towards the solution of today's engineering
problems, *if the speculations are used as a creative tool and
not as dogma*.

Seen that way, "the path to nanotechnology" isn't so important.
Technology will emerge out of what is going on today, and it may
at some point in the future converge on something like
Drexler's vision, or we may in the meantime come up with something
better than Drexer nanotech and bypass it altogether.

Problem is, just as with the old Von Braun religious sacraments 
of shuttle/space station/planetary base, which
the NASA/NSS axis keep practicing regardless of any clues from
reality, in some quarters nanotechnology is becoming a set
of rituals, "thou shalt build thy machines just so", which
for now is merely silly, but can like much of NASA become destructive
and corrupting once the nanopork barrel starts to roll.

