From: mcpherso@lumina.ucsd.edu (John McPherson)
Subject: SPEECH: Judeo-Christian ethics

   Centered on the question "Have Judeo-Christian ethics advanced or
degraded civilization?" was a joint-meeting between the Humanist
Fellowship and Liberty Toastmasters of San Diego which took place
Thursday night.

   I was one of 6 speakers, and this notice is to inform you that
copies of my speech are available.  If you'd like a copy, (about 
9 Kbytes in length), please send me an email request.
If I get more than 10 requests, I'll just go ahead and post it.

   The viewpoint I present in the speech is basically that: what has
advanced civilization the most is reason, what has degraded it the most
is force, and that compared to those influences, J-C ethics per se 
haven't had much effect one way or the other.  I tried to orient my 
speech to a particular audience (humanists and libertarians), and
I also included a recap of the history of Western Civilization, with
special interest in the Age of the Enlightenment.

   About the other speakers:
#1 -- a atheistic humanist (and anarchist) basically trashed J-C ethics, 
   but delivered a hilarious comparison of Eden with the welfare state
   that even the Christian speaker enjoyed.
#2 -- me (atheist and "free-market liberal" :-), voting NOTA.
#3 -- an agnostic Republican who praised J-C ethics as the foundation
   of our Constitution. (He got some flack for that)
#4 -- a libertarian humanist quoting Ayn Rand and praising ancient
   Greek civilization, who tried to be gentle while trashing J-C ethics.
#5 -- a Christian libertarian who did his best to defend J-C ethics,
   and to trash Statism for giving it a bad name.
#6 -- a libertarian who seemed to favor the J-C ethics, but proposed
   that instead of 10 commandments we should only have 3:  The Rights
   to Life, Liberty, and Property.

   About 20-25 people attended, and overall it was an entertaining
and stimulating evening.  I'd recommend such joint-meetings for those
who enjoy group activities.

John McPherson (mcpherso@lumina.ucsd.edu)

